---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';

const structuredData = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'What to check before installing a website blocker extension',
  description:
    'A practical checklist for website blocker permissions, privacy, bypasses, schedules, and pricing.',
  dateModified: '2026-08-24',
};
---

<ContentLayout
  title="What to check before installing a website blocker extension"
  metaTitle="Choosing a website blocker extension"
  description="Choose a website blocker extension by comparing permissions, privacy, bypass behavior, schedules, accounts, analytics, and pricing."
  eyebrow="Buyer’s guide"
  intro="The largest blocker is not automatically the best blocker. Start with the boundary you need, then choose the smallest permission and feature set that can enforce it."
  updated="August 24, 2026"
  structuredData={structuredData}
  toc={[
    { href: '#blocking-model', label: 'Choose the blocking model' },
    { href: '#permissions', label: 'Read the permissions' },
    { href: '#data', label: 'Follow the data' },
    { href: '#features', label: 'Separate useful features from overhead' },
    { href: '#checklist', label: 'Installation checklist' },
  ]}
>
  <section id="blocking-model">
    <h2>First choose the kind of boundary</h2>
    <p>
      “Website blocker” describes several different products. One may keep a domain closed
      until you deliberately re-enable it. Another may allow twenty minutes per day. A third
      may coordinate scheduled sessions across every device you own.
    </p>
    <div class="choice-grid">
      <article>
        <p class="eyebrow">Persistent list</p>
        <h3>Open or blocked</h3>
        <p>Best when a handful of sites simply should not open in this browser.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="eyebrow">Time allowance</p>
        <h3>Limited, not forbidden</h3>
        <p>Best when the site is useful but you want a daily ceiling.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="eyebrow">Focus system</p>
        <h3>Scheduled sessions</h3>
        <p>Best when routines, hard locks, applications, and multiple devices matter.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="permissions">
    <h2>Read the permission prompt as a product description</h2>
    <p>
      Permissions reveal architecture. An extension that modifies YouTube recommendations or
      measures time on a page needs deeper page access than one that hands Chrome a list of
      domains to refuse.
    </p>
    <p>
      Broad access is not automatically malicious; it may be necessary for the advertised
      feature. The important question is whether you want that feature enough to grant that
      access. A trustworthy listing explains the trade rather than hiding it behind “we value
      your privacy.”
    </p>
    <aside class="callout">
      <strong>Small is verifiable</strong>
      <p>
        torwache declares three permissions: local storage, declarative network rules, and a
        navigation signal used to show a readable local block page. It declares no host
        permissions and ships no content scripts.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section id="data">
    <h2>Follow the blocklist and the browsing history</h2>
    <p>
      A blocklist can reveal sensitive habits. Syncing it is convenient, but it also moves that
      list from local browser storage to an account or cloud service. Usage dashboards require
      another category of data: which sites were open, and for how long.
    </p>
    <p>
      Decide whether cross-device convenience or analytics is worth that data model. If it is,
      choose a provider whose policy clearly states retention and deletion. If it is not, choose
      a blocker that does not retain usage history in the first place.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="features">
    <h2>Useful feature or another thing to maintain?</h2>
    <dl class="decision-list">
      <div>
        <dt>Schedules</dt>
        <dd>
          Valuable for recurring work hours; unnecessary when the same sites are always blocked.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Password protection</dt>
        <dd>
          Useful for parental controls or accountability partners; friction for personal lists.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Usage analytics</dt>
        <dd>
          Useful when you want to study behavior; overhead when you already know the distracting
          sites.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Cross-device sync</dt>
        <dd>Useful across phones and computers; unnecessary for a short list in one browser.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Bypass button</dt>
        <dd>
          Useful for accidental blocks; counterproductive when the point is to stop negotiating.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section id="checklist">
    <h2>A 60-second installation checklist</h2>
    <ul class="check-list numbered-checks">
      <li><span>01</span> Does it block the way you need: permanent, limited, or scheduled?</li>
      <li><span>02</span> Can you explain why it needs every requested permission?</li>
      <li><span>03</span> Is the blocklist local, synced, or tied to an account?</li>
      <li><span>04</span> Does “free” mean free, a trial, or a restricted tier?</li>
      <li><span>05</span> Can you remove your data and export your settings?</li>
      <li>
        <span>06</span> Does the block page support the boundary or immediately undermine it?
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For concrete differences, see the current comparisons with
      <a href="/compare/blocksite/">BlockSite</a>,
      <a href="/compare/stayfocusd/">StayFocusd</a>,
      <a href="/compare/cold-turkey/">Cold Turkey</a>, and
      <a href="/compare/freedom/">Freedom</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</ContentLayout>
